WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.138
A small company's program records card payments for coffee shops.

2
00:00:07.138 --> 00:00:13.145
It also writes a line about every payment to a log file, and the log has grown to 20 GB.

3
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.968
One evening the disk is 98 percent full, so someone deletes the log.

4
00:00:17.968 --> 00:00:19.849
It vanishes from the folder.

5
00:00:19.849 --> 00:00:22.401
And the disk is still 98 percent full.

6
00:00:22.633 --> 00:00:25.131
So what did delete actually remove?

7
00:00:25.131 --> 00:00:26.487
Hold that question.

8
00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:29.452
It's easy to think a file is its name.

9
00:00:29.452 --> 00:00:34.624
A file browser shows it that way: one name, one icon, and delete takes both.

10
00:00:34.833 --> 00:00:37.239
So look at what has to be stored.

11
00:00:37.239 --> 00:00:41.759
A disk is a long row of equal pieces called blocks, 4 KB each.

12
00:00:42.033 --> 00:00:54.157
For every file, the file system, the part of the operating system that looks after files, records which blocks hold its bytes, its size, its owner, who may read it, and when it last changed.

13
00:00:54.433 --> 00:00:59.133
The simplest design keeps all of that next to the name: one row per file.

14
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:03.137
It breaks on the first file you want under two names.

15
00:01:03.137 --> 00:01:07.861
The payments program keeps the year's payments in ledger.csv, 2 GB.

16
00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:13.626
An auditor wants the same file as ledger-2026.csv, without a copy.

17
00:01:13.626 --> 00:01:16.305
Now two rows must agree forever.

18
00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:22.017
Add one payment through ledger.csv, and its row grows by 31 bytes.

19
00:01:22.017 --> 00:01:24.495
The other row still says 2 GB.

20
00:01:24.767 --> 00:01:26.353
Forever didn't last long.

21
00:01:26.353 --> 00:01:30.860
We want one place that holds the file, and names that only point to it.

22
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.290
Libraries solved this long ago.

23
00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:39.021
Every book has a number on its spine, and a catalogue card says: this title, book 13.

24
00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:45.173
One book can have a card under its title and another under its author, and neither card is the book.

25
00:01:45.467 --> 00:01:47.665
Linux splits the row the same way.

26
00:01:47.665 --> 00:01:57.107
Everything about the file moves into one box with a number and no name: size, owner, permissions, times, a count of links, and the list of blocks.

27
00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:58.983
That's the numbered book.

28
00:01:58.983 --> 00:02:01.879
It's called an inode, short for index node.

29
00:02:02.133 --> 00:02:07.262
The folder keeps only the cards: each entry is a name and an inode number.

30
00:02:07.262 --> 00:02:09.549
Linux calls a folder a directory.

31
00:02:09.767 --> 00:02:19.096
To open /tmp/ledger.csv, the kernel, the core of the operating system, reads the top directory and finds tmp.

32
00:02:19.333 --> 00:02:22.983
It reads tmp and finds ledger.csv, 13.

33
00:02:22.983 --> 00:02:25.192
Then it reads inode 13.

34
00:02:25.467 --> 00:02:32.257
Here the library picture breaks: a library has one flat catalogue, but folders sit inside folders.

35
00:02:32.633 --> 00:02:34.720
Now the auditor's name is easy.

36
00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.412
Add a second entry with the same number.

37
00:02:37.412 --> 00:02:38.691
That's a hard link.

38
00:02:38.933 --> 00:02:41.472
The inode counts its entries: links, 2.

39
00:02:41.472 --> 00:02:46.093
Add a payment through either name, and there's only one size to change.

40
00:02:46.333 --> 00:02:50.357
There's a second kind of link: a card that says, see under Ledger.

41
00:02:50.357 --> 00:02:52.491
That's a symbolic link, or symlink.

42
00:02:52.767 --> 00:03:01.904
latest.csv is an inode of its own, number 12, and its content is a path, the 10 bytes ledger.csv.

43
00:03:02.133 --> 00:03:06.044
The kernel looks that name up again every time the link is opened.

44
00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.603
Now delete ledger.csv.

45
00:03:08.603 --> 00:03:15.314
Afterwards, which name still works: ledger-2026.csv, or latest.csv?

46
00:03:15.314 --> 00:03:16.916
Pause and guess.

47
00:03:20.167 --> 00:03:23.913
The entry is gone, and links drops to 1.

48
00:03:23.913 --> 00:03:29.439
ledger-2026.csv still leads to inode 13, every byte intact.

49
00:03:29.667 --> 00:03:34.525
latest.csv looks up ledger.csv, finds nothing, and fails.

50
00:03:34.525 --> 00:03:36.912
And not one block was freed.

51
00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:40.431
So rm, the delete command, doesn't really delete.

52
00:03:40.431 --> 00:03:46.365
It sends the kernel a request called unlink: remove one entry, and lower the count by one.

53
00:03:46.633 --> 00:03:48.512
rm sounds like a shredder.

54
00:03:48.512 --> 00:03:51.041
It's really an eraser for one card.

55
00:03:51.433 --> 00:04:03.728
The kernel frees a file only when the count reaches 0 and no program still has it open, like a library that throws a book out only when no card points to it and nobody has it borrowed.

56
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:07.850
Delete ledger-2026.csv too, and the count is 0.

57
00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:13.912
Nobody has it open, so the kernel frees it, and its blocks are free again.

58
00:04:14.167 --> 00:04:15.405
Now our full disk.

59
00:04:15.405 --> 00:04:22.287
The log lost its last name, but the payments program still had it open, writing a line every second.

60
00:04:22.533 --> 00:04:23.575
It's borrowed.

61
00:04:23.575 --> 00:04:25.063
So all 20 GB stayed.

62
00:04:25.063 --> 00:04:29.081
Later in the lesson, we'll find that file and free it.

63
00:04:29.467 --> 00:04:31.054
Two limits on hard links.

64
00:04:31.054 --> 00:04:35.880
A hard link holds only a number, so it can't reach into another file system.

65
00:04:36.133 --> 00:04:40.385
Book 13 in another library is a different book, maybe a cookbook.

66
00:04:40.667 --> 00:04:46.668
And Linux refuses hard links to directories, which could put a folder inside itself, a loop.

67
00:04:47.033 --> 00:04:48.958
So, what did delete remove?

68
00:04:48.958 --> 00:04:49.600
One name.

69
00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:58.296
The file is its inode, names are cards that point to it, and the kernel frees it only when no card and no program is left.

70
00:04:58.767 --> 00:05:00.168
One quick check.

71
00:05:00.168 --> 00:05:03.496
Someone makes a new, empty ledger.csv.

72
00:05:03.496 --> 00:05:05.598
Now you open latest.csv.

73
00:05:05.598 --> 00:05:06.999
What do you get?

74
00:05:06.999 --> 00:05:08.400
Pause and think.

75
00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:12.973
The new, empty file.

76
00:05:12.973 --> 00:05:18.397
A symlink holds a name, not a number, so it follows whatever has that name now.
